@page "/panel-grid"

<CatHead Title="Panel grid">
    Panels each placed on cells of a grid — a column, a row, a width and a height — that the reader
    moves and resizes, and the app stores and renders back. For panels you place yourself, use the
    <a href="/grid">24-column grid</a>.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.PanelGrid.PanelGrid" Title="Panels placed on cells" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Panels each placed on cells: <code>--panel-x</code> and <code>--panel-y</code> are the column and
        the row it starts on, from 0, and <code>--panel-w</code> and <code>--panel-h</code> how many it
        covers, written inline from the arrangement you store. <code>--panel-cols</code> (12) columns
        share the width and a row is <code>--panel-row</code> (3rem) tall. A panel stays where it is put,
        so a narrow one can sit beside a tall one under a wide one &mdash; and nothing stops two
        overlapping but the arrangement, which <code>SednaPanels.Arrange</code> settles.
    </p>
    <p>
        Narrower than 560px the panels stack, a whole row wide, in reading order.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.PanelGridArrange" Title="Panels the reader arranges" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; drag a panel by its grip, pull its side, its bottom or its corner.</strong>
        The grid is a drag zone with <code>data-drag-axis="cells"</code>, arranged while it carries
        <code>data-arrange</code>: outside it the panels are content, and their grips and handles are not
        there. A panel in the way of the one carried goes down below it and every panel floats up, so
        nothing overlaps and nothing hangs over a gap; the grid marks the cell it would land on.
        <code>sedna-drop</code> and <code>sedna-resize</code> carry <code>Panels</code>, the whole
        arrangement as <code>SednaPanel</code>s in reading order: store it and render it back. Nothing is
        moved for you &mdash; the preview stands until your render arrives.
    </p>
    <p>
        The handles count in cells, and their <code>aria-valuemin</code> is a panel's least size. The camera
        carries <code>data-panel-ratio="16:9"</code>, so whichever handle moves it keeps that shape to the
        nearest row.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<h2 id="arranging-from-the-keyboard">Arranging from the keyboard <CatAnchor Id="arranging-from-the-keyboard" /></h2>
<div class="sedna-scroll-x">
    <table class="table">
        <thead>
            <tr><th>On</th><th>Key</th><th>Does</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>a panel's grip</td><td><kbd class="kbd">Space</kbd> <kbd class="kbd">Enter</kbd></td><td>Picks the panel up; while it is held, drops it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>a held panel</td><td><kbd class="kbd">←</kbd> <kbd class="kbd">→</kbd></td><td>A column towards the inline start or end, mirrored right to left.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>a held panel</td><td><kbd class="kbd">↑</kbd> <kbd class="kbd">↓</kbd></td><td>To the next row where it lands somewhere else: above or below the panel in the way.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>a held panel</td><td><kbd class="kbd">Esc</kbd>, or leaving it</td><td>Puts it back.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>a side or bottom handle</td><td>the arrows along it</td><td>A cell wider or narrower, taller or shorter; four with <kbd class="kbd">Shift</kbd>.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>
    Each step is read out through your <code>[data-drag-live]</code> region, as on
    <a href="/drag-drop#keyboard">drag and drop</a>; on a panel grid <code>{column}</code> and
    <code>{row}</code> name the cell, from 1. A handle is a separator whose <code>aria-valuenow</code> you
    render from the arrangement, so its new size is read when your render arrives.
    <code>sednaUi.panels.arrange(panels, columns, moved)</code> is the same layout in script, for a page
    without Blazor.
</p>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.DashboardTiles" Title="Panels the reader reorders" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; drag a panel by its grip, or pull its bottom edge.</strong> A dashboard the
        reader puts in order is a <a href="/drag-drop"><code>.drag-grid</code></a> of
        <a href="/card"><code>.card--resizable</code></a> panels, with nothing added:
        <code>data-drag-axis="grid"</code> numbers the panels in reading order, and the app moves one
        in its own list on <code>sedna-drop</code>; each panel's <code>.card-resize</code> writes
        <code>--card-height</code>, and the app keeps it from <code>sedna-resize</code>. Store both per
        reader and render them back. <code>--drag-grid-min</code> on the grid is the narrowest a panel
        may be, which sets how many fit side by side. Where a panel needs a width of its own and a
        place beside and under others, it is a <code>.panel-grid</code>, above.
    </p>
</CatExample>
